WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.480
Good evidence is current, relevant, traceable, complete enough,

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.900
and tied to a specific control or assertion.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:11.440
Current evidence reflects the system as it operates now or during the period being

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:12.540
reviewed.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.960
Relevant evidence supports the specific claim under review.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:20.660
Traceable evidence can be connected back to the system, component, owner, control,

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.520
finding, or operational activity.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:27.740
Complete enough means the evidence lets a reviewer understand what happened without

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:28.840
guesswork.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:30.980
Evidence can take many forms.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.080
Configuration records can show how systems are set up.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:35.980
Logs can show activity.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.520
Screenshots can be useful at a high level when they are dated, contextualized,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.420
and tied to a system state.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.920
Tickets can show workflow, ownership, and closure.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.540
Scan results can show vulnerability status.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:54.280
Meeting records, approvals, access reviews, test results, monitoring outputs,

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.340
and operational records can all support different kinds of assertions.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.400
Weak evidence creates friction.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:02.940
Vague assurances do not show what happened.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:06.760
Stale artifacts may describe a system that no longer exists.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:10.500
Unsupported statements ask reviewers to trust without a record.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.800
Evidence that cannot be tied back to the system may be impossible to evaluate.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.060
A screenshot with no date, owner, environment,

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.400
or explanation may create more questions than it answers.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.340
Evidence quality matters as much as evidence quantity.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.400
Good evidence is also protected.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:31.740
Security documentation can reveal architecture, configuration, vulnerabilities,

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.120
privileged processes, dependencies, and operational practices.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:41.460
Evidence repositories need access control, version tracking, retention expectations,

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.320
and handling rules appropriate to sensitivity.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:48.860
Evidence should be reviewable by the right people, but it should not be casually exposed.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.560
Trustworthy evidence is useful, understandable, and responsibly managed.
